+    <section class="journal" id="journal">
+      <h2>Roaster's Journal</h2>
+      <div class="journal-text" id="journal-text">
+        <p>Every batch we roast begins months before the beans ever reach our shop, usually in a conversation with a
+          farmer we have come to know by name. We buy directly from small family-owned farms and cooperatives in
+          Ethiopia, Colombia, Guatemala, and Sumatra, and we visit each of them at least once every two years. Those
+          trips are how we learn what a given harvest actually tastes like, how the farm is treating its land and its
+          workers, and whether our purchasing is helping or straining the community around it.</p>
+        <p>Before we commit to a lot, we cup dozens of samples at our roastery. Samples arrive in small sealed bags, and
+          we taste them blind, scoring each on aroma, acidity, body, sweetness, and finish. Only coffees that score
+          above 85 points and that our whole tasting panel can agree on make it to the next stage. It is slow, and we
+          turn down far more coffee than we buy, but it keeps the menu honest.</p>
+        <p>Price matters just as much as flavor. We pay a fixed premium well above the Fairtrade minimum, agreed on
+          before the harvest, so farmers know what they will earn even if the market drops. Where possible we sign
+          multi-year contracts, which gives growers the confidence to invest in shade trees, better drying beds, and
+          water-saving equipment. Roughly a third of our green coffee budget goes to these long-term relationships.</p>
+        <p>Once the beans are on the water, we track them until they land at our dock. Green coffee is stored in
+          humidity-controlled bins and rested for a few weeks before roasting, then roasted in small batches of no more
+          than twelve kilograms. We roast to order each week, so what you drink is almost always less than ten days off
+          the roaster. Seasonal changes are part of the story: when a harvest ends, so does that coffee, and we would
+          rather say goodbye to a favorite than serve a tired version of it.</p>
+      </div>
